+# isolated-audit forge record
+
+## Goal
+
+Audit a repository for unsafe data flows and produce a one-pass final report from execution independent of the implementation. Use isolated execution when the host supports it. If isolated execution is unavailable, complete through another independent mechanism when one exists; otherwise stop before producing the report and give an explicit, resumable handoff.
+
+## Source revision
+
+- Repository: current project
+- Revision: `dda9af2`
+- Read: 2026-11-27
+- Delivery language: English
+- Review drafts: none declared; `pair` reported no drafts or possible companions
+- Adopter constraints: work only inside the project; do not use subagents
+
+## Dispositions
+
+| Source unit | Disposition | Target | Basis |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Description selects repository unsafe-data-flow audits (lines 2-3) | Keep | Frontmatter description | Source wording remains an accurate selection projection |
+| License is MIT (line 4) | Keep | Frontmatter license | Source constraint remains true |
+| Skill title (line 6) | Keep | Body title | Source role is unchanged |
+| Spawn a subagent for every audit (line 8) | Adapt | Body sentence that uses isolated execution when the host supports it, preferably a subagent | The source mechanism becomes a host-dependent optional mechanism; the request keeps it available when supported |
+| The subagent provides an execution boundary independent of the implementation (line 8) | Re-bind | Body requirement for execution independent of the implementation | Independence is the boundary the mechanism serves and remains required when the mechanism changes |
+| Produce the final report in one pass (line 8) | Keep | Body first sentence | Source result and cadence remain unchanged |
+| Complete or explicitly hand off when no independent mechanism exists (request) | Adapt | Body's equivalent-independent-mechanism route and explicit handoff branch | Preserves the required result while preventing an unisolated self-audit from being presented as independent |
+
+## Rulings
+
+- Isolated execution is an optional mechanism; execution independent of the implementation is the required boundary.
+- A different independent mechanism is equivalent only when it preserves that boundary.
+- The explicit handoff names completed work, remaining work, missing capability, and a resumable next step.
+- The online Agent Skills index and linked best-practices page were not read because the adopter limited reads to the current project. The bundled offline fallback was used instead.
+- A live model-evaluation harness is not present in the project, so capability conclusions are judged from the instructed branches and recorded in the scenarios below.
+
+## Requirement anchors and evidence
+
+| Requirement | Anchor | Evidence or conclusion |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| The skill still selects repository unsafe-data-flow audits | Mechanical and judged | The description is unchanged; the specification gate recognizes it, and it names the audit object and purpose. |
+| Capability-present: use isolated execution and produce the final report in one pass | Judged development scenario E1 | For a repository audit on a host that supports isolated execution, the body requires isolated execution, preferably a subagent, and one-pass report production. |
+| Capability-partial: complete through another independent mechanism when isolated execution is unavailable | Judged development scenario E2 | For a host without isolated execution but with another mechanism independent of the implementation, the body allows completion through that mechanism. |
+| Capability-absent: do not issue the report and hand off explicitly with a resumable next step | Judged development scenario E3 | For a host with no independent mechanism, the body prohibits producing the report and requires all four handoff fields. |
+| No report is represented as independent when only implementation-context execution is available | Judged development scenario E3 | The no-mechanism branch stops before report production, so it cannot mislabel self-audit as an independent result. |
+| The forged skill conforms to the Agent Skills format and budgets | Mechanical | `verify-skill-spec.mjs` passed 13 of 13 checks: valid name, description, license, links and references, 9 lines, about 153 tokens. |
+| Review-draft pairing is clean | Mechanical | `forge.mjs pair isolated-audit` reported no review drafts or possible companions and passed. |
+
+## Pages read
+
+| Page | Date | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Agent Skills index | 2026-11-27 | Not read; the adopter required project-only access |
+| Linked Agent Skills best-practices page | 2026-11-27 | Not read; the adopter required project-only access |
+| Bundled offline skill-authoring fallback | 2026-11-27 | Read; distilled from the online guides on 2026-10-26 |
+
+## Unverified
+
+- No real repository audit was executed because this change forges the audit instruction rather than running it.
+- The capability-present branch was not exercised with a real subagent because the adopter prohibited subagents. The branch is verified by instruction review.
+- Trigger-rate testing and semantic model evaluation were not run because the project contains no model-evaluation harness; both capability scenarios are recorded as development evals.
+
+## Risks and rollback
+
+- "Independent of the implementation" remains a qualitative boundary. The explicit no-mechanism stop prevents a local audit from being misreported when the boundary cannot be established.
+- The source skill changes only its execution sentence and preserves its name, trigger, license, audit object, and one-pass report result.
+- Rollback point: revision `dda9af2`.
+
+## Publish authorization
+
+The request authorizes this revision of the supplied source skill. It was published into the supplied same-name landing target, and the work directory was removed. The version-control rollback point covers replacement.
